00:00:03.990 --> 00:00:06.230 align:start position:0%
it's almost six months since sudan's  military seized power and the opposition  remains vocal on wednesday protesters in  the capital khartoum and several other  cities once again took to the streets to  voice their anger at the military and  demand they hand over power to a  civilian government  we came out to send a message to the  military who are in power around the  country that we want a pure civilian  government the army should return to the  barracks no militia can rule a country  and i think that is a very clear message  protesters are also commemorating the  third anniversary of a sitting in front  of the army headquarters that sit-in led  the military to depose long-time  president ahmad al-bashir after months  of protests and signed a power-sharing  agreement with the protest movement an  agreement that was dissolved when the  army took over last october  we made a huge achievement in 2019 and  ended a regime stronger than the current  one they say the revolution was stolen  but it wasn't the army was in  partnership with us and they betrayed us  and this is a fresh revolution with no  partnership with the military so now we  want a civilian government without the  military we can't live under a military  rule  security forces used tear gas and live  ammunition to disperse protesters in the  past six months more than 90 protesters  have been killed and hundreds injured as  security forces use what rights groups  and the un describe as excessive and  brutal force against unarmed  demonstrators  last year's military takeover has  created a political crisis there hasn't  been a prime minister for three months  and the caretaker government is still  running the day-to-day affairs of the  country the uncertainty along with donor  countries halting financial aid sudan  following the takeover has created an  economic crisis but the army says even  with protests against its rule it won't  hand over power except to an elected  government  a joint effort by the united nations  african union and the regional  intergovernmental authority on  development or egad to facilitate talks  between the various political actors in  sudan was launched in january but many  protesters have rejected the process  this revolution will succeed whether  that happens today or tomorrow or next  year or the coming generation in 2019 we  demand the end of the regime we're  demanding that same thing now  we don't want partnerships or  negotiations or compromise with the  military  protesters have faced the threat of  violence and detention from security  forces but they say until their demands  for a civilian government and a return  to a democratic transition are met they  won't stop protesting heber morgan  al-jazeera khartoum
 

